In Java 8, the most significant change in memory management is the replacement
of Permanent Generation (PermGen) with Metaspace.
Metaspace, unlike PermGen, is not part of the Java heap and is allocated
 from native memory.
This change offers more flexibility in handling class metadata and
reduces the likelihood of OutOfMemoryError related to PermGen's fixed size.

PermGen Removal:
Java 8 eliminated PermGen, which was a fixed-size memory area used to
store class metadata, interned strings, and other static information.
Metaspace Introduction:
Metaspace replaced PermGen, and it's a dynamically expanding memory area,
allocated from native memory (outside the Java heap).
Dynamic Sizing:
Metaspace automatically adjusts its size based on the application's needs,
unlike PermGen, which had a fixed size and could lead to OutOfMemoryError
when it ran out of space
Heap Space:
The Java heap itself is still divided into Young Generation (for new objects)
and Old Generation (for long-lived objects)

